This small closure keeps a private counter.
```javascript
function makeCounter() {
  let count = 0;
  return { next() { count += 1; return count; } };
}
const a = makeCounter();
const b = makeCounter();
console.log(a.next(), a.next(), b.next());
```
It prints 1 2 1. The two objects have separate count variables, just as two note histories should remain separate.

Editing after undo creates a new branch.
```javascript
let versions = ["A", "B", "C"];
let cursor = 1;
versions = versions.slice(0, cursor + 1);
versions.push("D");
cursor = versions.length - 1;
console.log(versions, versions[cursor]);
```
The versions are A, B, D and the current text is D. slice excludes its end index, so cursor + 1 keeps the current version before appending.

after:
This small example shows how separate closures keep separate values.
```javascript
function makeCounter() {
  let count = 0;
  return { next() { count += 1; return count; } };
}
const a = makeCounter();
const b = makeCounter();
console.log(a.next(), a.next(), b.next());
```
Output:
```text
1 2 1
```
Calling `a.next()` twice changes only `a`'s count. `b` still starts at 1. Our note histories should stay separate in the same way.

A new edit after undo replaces the old future versions.
```javascript
let versions = ["A", "B", "C"];
let cursor = 1;
versions = versions.slice(0, cursor + 1);
versions.push("D");
cursor = versions.length - 1;
console.log(versions, versions[cursor]);
```
The saved versions are now A, B, D. The current text is D.

`slice(0, cursor + 1)` keeps everything through the current version. `push` adds the new one. The cursor then moves to that last entry.
